0/evidence/D_terminal.md:147:| TUI-R6 | Default approval posture is an explicit, documented decision; first run in an untrusted workspace states the mode and how to change it | safety (TUI-07) | RELEASE-REQUIRED | PTY first-run screen contains mode + switch hint; docs match |\ndocs/v1.0.0/evidence/C_tools_security_persistence.md:37:  - `.nexus-security.json` is read from the untrusted workspace (security.ts:48). It can only add entries, except that `allowedDomains` narrows egress and `allowExternalSymlinks` is never consumed.\ndocs/reviews/NEXUS_APPLICATION_MATURITY_REVIEW_2026-10-12.md:21:**Acceptance evidence:** negative tests proving untrusted workspace configuration cannot silently redirect context; positive tests for explicit operator approval; end-to-end audit/receipt naming destination/provider/model and data class; enforceable spend/request limits.\ndocs/PHASE2_RECONCILED_REQUIREMENTS.md:776:| **F-04** | Automatic execution of untrusted workspace plugins (Demonstrated) | **EXT-002, EXT-003** | Fully resolved incorporating Operator Decision 2 (policy-based execution authority). |\ndocs/PHASE2S_SECURITY_AND_DATA_REVIEW.md:330:  5. Phase 2 Section 11 classifies plugins as `INDEPENDENT -> BOUNDED or remain INDEPENDENT`, but fails to forbid automatic execution of untrusted workspace-local code.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a16e34-cb69-7fb3-96f2-8eec5d8c48cb","create_time":1792850640.1971416}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
